Kevin Hassett has been named by President Donald Trump to head the White House National Economic Council (NEC).  The appointment follows Trump’s statement that Hassett will help secure fair trade with countries that have historically taken advantage of the United States and strengthen economic ties with both new and existing allies.

Hassett, 62, is a research fellow at the Hoover Institution at Stanford University.  He previously served as chair of the White House Council of Economic Advisers (CEA) from September 2017 to mid‑2019 and returned to the White House in early 2020 to assist in mitigating the economic impact of the COVID‑19 pandemic.  He has also been an assistant professor at Columbia University and a member of the American Enterprise Institute.

During his tenure with the Trump administration, Hassett frequently used positive economic assessments to support the president’s trade policies and counter data that critics said undermined the administration’s economic performance.  In an interview with CNBC in August, he defended Trump’s tariff strategy, arguing that the president sought to set U.S. tariffs at levels comparable to those imposed by other countries on U.S. goods.  He also noted that Trump was “very unhappy” when other nations taxed American manufactured products.

Hassett has publicly emphasized the importance of an independent central bank.  In a October interview with the Financial Times, he said the Federal Reserve had “let inflation get out of control” and that maintaining central‑bank independence was crucial.

The NEC, which Hassett will now lead, is often confused with the CEA.  The NEC was established during Bill Clinton’s presidential campaign, reportedly inspired by the slogan “idiot! problem is the economy,” and is tasked with coordinating economic policy.  It must navigate internal and external pressures that can influence presidential decision‑making.  Hassett’s new role will involve close collaboration with the Treasury nominee and hedge‑fund manager Benson to implement many of Trump’s unfulfilled economic promises.
