During the COVID‑19 pandemic a wealthy British businessman, Gareth Wilson, 45, built a luxury five‑bedroom house on land in North Ayrshire without obtaining planning permission. The house, which featured sandstone exterior walls, a Spanish slate roof, a spiral staircase, tiered balconies and a spa centre with sauna and steam room, was deemed a breach of local planning regulations and was ordered for demolition by the council.

Wilson applied for retrospective permission in 2023, but the application was rejected. After further negotiations, he reached an agreement with the North Ayrshire Council and a new development plan was approved. The revised scheme incorporates the existing house and six new holiday cottages, and adds vehicle access, a parking area, a footpath and a horse path to improve the site’s tourism facilities.

The council said the proposal aligns with the local development plan and will deliver positive economic and social benefits. It also highlighted that the Tennox Farm development will enhance local tourism infrastructure, protect and enhance the rural environment, and provide high‑quality holiday accommodation to meet growing demand.

While the council supports the project, the Coal Authority has expressed concerns about potential risks from past mining activity in the area and has called for an investigation. The approved development must be carried out in accordance with the submitted plans, drawings, recommendations and all supporting documents.
