Bloomberg opinion columnist Lisa Javis, who focuses on biotechnology, healthcare and pharmaceuticals and formerly served as executive editor of *Chemical & Engineering News*, has written about the United States confronting its most severe measles outbreak in over three decades. The outbreak has seen 935 confirmed cases across 30 states and three deaths, the first measles‑related fatalities in the country in a decade.

The article criticizes U.S. Health and Human Services Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. for spreading misinformation and unproven therapies that divert public‑health policy from evidence‑based medicine. Kennedy has declined to acknowledge that vaccination is the only proven method to prevent measles. He has promoted vitamin A as a treatment, a strategy that has reduced complications in low‑income countries but may be ineffective in the United States. In the weeks after Kennedy began advocating vitamin A, a Texas hospital reported several children hospitalized for liver toxicity caused by excessive vitamin A intake. A survey conducted in March 2024 found that 20 % of adults believed the claim that vitamin A prevents measles, and one‑third of adults had heard the rumor; the proportion had fallen to 18 % by March.

