Tibet earthquake highlights risks of large dam projects in seismic zone

A 6.8‑magnitude earthquake struck Tibet early this week, killing at least 126 people and damaging four reservoirs. The quake ranks fifth among earthquakes since 2008.

The event occurred in a region that already hosts 68 large dams that tap high‑altitude lakes and rivers, yet only about one‑fifth of the potential hydropower has been developed. Experts warn that seismic activity threatens these structures.

China’s planned “墨脱” mega‑dam, approved in December last year, would be more than three times the size of the Three Gorges Dam and is designed to generate 34 GW of clean energy, a key element of China’s 2030 carbon‑peak goal. The project has attracted criticism because it sits on active fault lines and would add enormous weight to the region’s geology.

Chinese officials have not provided details on the damage to the dam’s reservoirs, and the Ministry of Water Resources has not responded to Reuters’ request for comment. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs said the project had passed rigorous disaster‑prevention tests.

International experts, including German professor Wolfgang Schwanghart and Indian geologist C. P. Rajendran, have highlighted the vulnerability of Himalayan dams. A 2018 study estimated that about a quarter of projects in the upper Yarlung Tsangpo basin could suffer moderate to severe damage in future earthquakes. The 2015 Nepal earthquake caused a 20 % outage of the country’s hydropower for over a year.

The region has a long history of seismic risk. In 2008 a 8.0‑level quake killed nearly 70,000 people. From 2010 to 2022, five earthquakes between 6.5 and 7.1 in Qinghai, Sichuan, Yunnan and Gansu killed 3,699 people and injured tens of thousands.

While China is the world’s largest carbon emitter and India ranks third, both countries see large dams as a means to secure clean energy and stimulate economic growth. However, experts argue that the ecological impact and seismic risk make continued dam construction in the Himalayas unsustainable, especially given the high transmission costs and low local demand.

The recent earthquake underscores the tension between the urgent need for clean energy and the safety risks posed by building massive infrastructure in one of the world’s most seismically active regions.
