Yesterday, a 7.6‑magnitude shallow earthquake struck the Caribbean Sea north of Honduras, about 209 km offshore of the Cayman Islands. The United States Geological Survey (USGS) reported a depth of 10 km; the German Research Centre for Geosciences (GFZ) recorded a magnitude of 7.5. The Pacific Tsunami Warning Center (PTWC) initially warned that the quake could generate a tsunami, but the latest data show the threat has been lifted and no further danger is expected. The U.S. Tsunami Warning System had earlier issued alerts that waves up to 3 m might reach parts of Cuba and up to 1 m could affect Honduras and the Cayman Islands; the Cayman Islands government posted a warning on its website urging residents to move inland. Puerto Rico and the U.S. Virgin Islands also received tsunami warnings.

According to the PTWC, after roughly three hours the major threat has passed, though waves up to 0.3 m may still occur. The Taiwan Meteorological Administration has updated its tsunami information release guidelines. It notes that waves between 0.3 and 1 m can overturn pedestrians or vehicles. Residents who receive a tsunami alert should immediately move to higher ground and only return once the alert is lifted. The guidelines also advise, in emergencies, seeking sturdy buildings or roofs; if time allows, opening windows to let water flow through can reduce structural impact. If escape to higher ground is impossible, people should look for nearby high fixed objects or wear life jackets and climb onto large floating debris.

The administration also reminds that tsunamis can produce multiple waves; after the first wave, people should remain in safe locations until a “tsunami alert lifted” or “tsunami warning lifted” notice is issued before returning to coastal areas.

The PTWC warned that areas within 620 miles (998 km) of the epicenter—including the Cayman Islands, Jamaica, Cuba, Mexico, Honduras, the Bahamas, Belize, Haiti, Costa Rica, Panama, Nicaragua and Guatemala—should remain alert.
