On January 9, the Office of the National Committee for Disaster Reduction and the Ministry of Emergency Management, together with the Ministry of Commerce and the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology, coordinated a joint supply effort that delivered 10,000 tons of bio‑granules, 25 tons of brick tea and 5,000 emergency lamps to the earthquake‑affected areas of Shigatse, Tibet. Earlier, two batches of central relief supplies had already arrived in the region.

The Ministry of Commerce immediately activated its emergency plan, placing 627 key supply‑maintaining enterprises into emergency status and increasing the flow of goods. It secured 74.3 million tons of grain, oil, meat and vegetables, mobilised 2,000 tons of frozen pork, 1,600 tons of frozen beef and mutton, and 2,250 tons of border‑sale tea from the central reserve. In cooperation with the Ministry of Emergency Management, the China Aid Foundation, Tencent Foundation, the Foundation Disaster Relief Coordination Committee and the central reserve tea‑storage enterprises, 50,000 jin of border‑sale tea was transported to the disaster zone to help residents keep warm during winter.

Local commerce authorities, using a joint supply‑and‑distribution mechanism, dispatched 153,000 cases of instant noodles, bottled water, bread, tsampa, ghee and other food items, as well as 73,000 pieces of electric blankets, heating stoves, quilts, winter coats and blankets. They also coordinated catering firms to provide hot meals for displaced residents and rescue workers, ensuring basic living needs were met.

Charitable organisations contributed heavily to the relief effort. The Chinese Charity Association donated 2 million yuan for immediate rescue and livelihood support; the Tencent Charity Foundation launched a 20 million yuan donation to cover emergency aid, temporary accommodation, winter supplies, post‑disaster reconstruction and employment‑generation projects. Other foundations—including the China Women’s Development Foundation, the China Primary Health Care Foundation, the Alibaba Charity Foundation, the SF Express Charity Foundation, the Bosideng Charity Foundation, the Xinhua Life Insurance Charity Foundation and the Star Cloud Cultural Education Foundation—donated emergency supplies, winter clothing or dispatched rescue teams. By 17:00 on January 8, online public‑fundraising platforms had attracted more than 1.7 million participants, with total donations and pledged funds exceeding 270 million yuan.

The Natural Resources Ministry’s emergency mapping team, equipped with drones and emergency maps, reached the epicentre in Dêrizê County that night. It identified 135 geological hazard points across ten towns in Dêrizê, Rêbû, Sêgê and Sa’ga counties, of which 39 were deemed unstable, 66 relatively stable and 30 stable. The team, supported by three cartographers from Sichuan Mapping and Information Bureau and Chongqing Mapping Institute, produced 24 satellite‑image comparison maps of the affected areas. These maps were supplied to the Tibet Disaster Relief Command and the Tibet Provincial Government for use in rescue operations.

Water‑engineering authorities maintained a fourth‑level emergency response. The Water Ministry and the Tibet Water Resources Department continued to monitor rainfall and potential damage to water infrastructure. No major dam failures or embankment breaches were reported, and main rivers showed no blockages or overtopping.

Overall, the coordinated response—combining government logistics, commercial supply chains, charitable donations, scientific mapping and water‑engineering monitoring—has kept the disaster‑affected communities supplied and safe while the long‑term reconstruction effort proceeds.
