**Central and Southern Taiwan Hit by Heavy Rain; Government Plans Recovery Measures**

Central and southern Taiwan have been battered by continuous heavy rain driven by a southwestern air flow. The flooding has already claimed three lives, left four people missing, and injured 49 others. According to the Fire Department briefing, 85 rescue incidents have been recorded, 153 people have been rescued, and four remain missing.

During a visit to the 0728 Heavy‑Rain Disaster Response Center on March 3, Executive Yuan Premier Chou Rong‑Tai announced that a special ordinance for the recovery of Typhoon Danas will be passed at the Executive Yuan meeting on Thursday, March 7. The ordinance will incorporate the recent 0728 heavy‑rain events and will prioritize the water‑management needs of affected areas, which will be incorporated into a new water‑management plan.

Premier Chou also outlined a four‑year plan worth 100 billion New Taiwan dollars to improve county and city river and drainage systems in response to climate change. He stressed that the plan will cover pumping equipment, source‑to‑end engineering, and legal measures.

The highest rainfall recorded in the Kaohsiung mountains exceeded 2,000 mm, prompting calls for close cooperation between central ministries, local governments, and civil‑society groups to provide necessary assistance and avoid further casualties. A disaster‑recovery command center has been established, headed by Executive Yuan political commissar Chen Jinde, and is already working on waste removal, environmental disinfection, and disease prevention to prevent a resurgence of dengue fever in the south.

A special budget will be submitted to the Legislative Yuan to support these efforts. The government will continue to strengthen its response to the immediate needs of residents while integrating long‑term water‑management solutions into its recovery strategy.
